AB  - Introduction: The increased demand for ophthalmological care in the SUS reflects the scarcity of this service provision in the country, especially due to the concentration of specialists in urban centers and a deficient and poorly coordinated comprehensive care network. University projects in partnership with the Ministry of Health allow the performance of retinography in primary care, using a portable retinograph operated locally by a trained health professional who captures the images, without the need for dilation and with the offer of remote diagnosis of patients, enabling early detection of diseases such as diabetic retinopathy, hypertensive retinopathy and glaucoma. Objective: To describe the performance of retinography in primary care patients and the resolution of these cases. Method: Cross-sectional study describing 2,158 consultations of the Teleretinography project, carried out between March 2023 and February 2024 in the state of Minas Gerais, Brazil, analyzing the demographic characteristics of the patients, the reasons for referral to telediagnosis, the characterization of the diagnoses made, as well as the resolution of the implemented telediagnosis model. A portable retinograph was used to capture color images that allow visualization of more peripheral areas with high quality, also with the support of Artificial Intelligence (AI) that signals areas of the retina with possible anomalies. Results: The patients were mostly female (67.7%), between 60 and 69 years of age (28.9%), and referred to telediagnosis mainly due to being diabetic (72%) and hypertensive (82.5%). Of the total, 45.1% had their ocular complaints completely resolved, without the need for referral to an ophthalmological consultation. In 39.1% of the patients, clinical conditions with altered diagnosis were detected, with the main findings being hypertensive retinopathy (44.8%) and diabetic retinopathy (27.5%). Conclusion: The study demonstrated that telemedicine can be a solution for less complex eye diseases, qualifying and helping to reduce waiting lists for ophthalmological care. It is a viable strategy for large-scale implementation in Primary Health Care, in order to minimize difficulties in accessing an ophthalmologist. The use of AI does not replace the ophthalmologist, but acts as a complementary tool in the diagnosis of the alterations found, contributing to the preparation of the report more quickly. © 2025 IADIS Press All rights reserved.
